    "media:imagetext": "P\nShanghai, China.\nNov. 6, 1938\nDearest Kits:\nSo much to say I hardly know where to start. I left Nanking Wednesday at\nsix in a hire car with two Chinese passengers and one other foreign passenger and\ntwo Chinese chauffeurs, each one nearly as big as Chillie Vaughn. It took us thirteen\nand one half hours to make Shanghai.\nIt was\nintensely interesting but I am sure\nyou would not have liked it, until you had got to Shanghai.\nThe road was narrow\nall the way, rainy and misty most of the way. The road was fairly good some of the\nway but muddy and rutty most of the way.\nThe two Chinese passengers spoke English\nand were very nice. We were stopped many times by soldiers and questioned but never\ndelayed. We took a road running south of Wusiph on the south side of Tahu Lake\nthat comes in to the Shanghai Hangehow road about two or three miles from Minghong.\nOf course all of such trips have abad reputation and Mr. Johnson will not give\nauthority for any one to make it. We skidded badly on one place where the road is\nnarrow and on top of a dyke about fifteen feet high and I thought we were going\nover surely. We were, but fortunately the side of the car smashed into a tree\nand helå us. The side of the car was badly smashed, glass broken, but far tunately\nit was shatter proof, the door mas jammed, fender smashed and top\nof car bent in,\nbut we didn't go over, and the frame of the chassis wasn't bent. We all got out,\ngot the car back in the road. I found out why the two big Chinese chauffeurs were\nused, and we went on.\nWe had to run in low and second so much we boiled out most\nof the water in the radiator and the engine got so hot it ran for five minutes with\nthe switch off. Finally stopped it by putting it in high gear with the brakes on.\nWe didn't have anything to get water in, dark as hell, but the car had one of the\nold style\nhorns with a big rubber bulb about as big as your head so the chauffeur\ntook it off and sucked up water in it from a wayside ditch and squirted in the\nradiator You can't beat the Chinese. We got stuck several times but managed to\npush ourselves out.\nWe struck the ferry west of Minghong about five A.M. at daybreak. The\nTerry came over, unloaded two new truck chassis and then turned around and went\nback empty.\nWe inquired as to what in the hell was going on and were told that\nthere were\nabout thirty trucks on the other side going to kanking and the drivers\nhad parked their trucks all over the road on the other side and dow both the loading\nand unloading runways so that it was impossible to unload anything on the other side.\nCan you imagine anything more typical than that, more unbelievable? I immediately\ndecided that was no place for us.\nWe were only\nabout fifteen miles or less from\nthe Front and if the weather that day was good\nall those new trucks standing there\nwould draw Jap planes like sugar drawing flies.\nIt took 35 minutes to make a round\ntrip on the ferry taking two trucks at a time and we were in for an eight hour wait.\nI got our other passengers together and told them I thought we should cross outselves\non the Engłisixerex empty ferry going back and wait on the other side for our car\nto get over.\nThere was no place to flop, no where to go, no protection at all and\nin case of\nattack it would have been a death trap. The other foreigner agreed at\nonce but the Chinese said the other side was like this side and we might loose our\nbaggage. I told nim I thought it was important to get over before flying started\nand not get caught on the ferry, and once on the other side we could walk down the",
